## Program.cs — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: route all logs to stderr so stdout stays clean for JSON-RPC
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient();  // registers IHttpClientFactory + HttpClient

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();      // discovers all [McpServerToolType] classes

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

## Tool class — wrapping a REST API

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    private const string BaseUrl = "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com";

    [McpServerTool, Description("Fetch a user by their numeric ID.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The user's numeric ID (1-10)")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        return await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"{BaseUrl}/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("List all posts, optionally filtered by user.")]
    public async Task<string> ListPosts(
        [Description("Filter posts to this user ID (omit for all)")] int? userId = null,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var url = userId.HasValue
            ? $"{BaseUrl}/posts?userId={userId}"
            : $"{BaseUrl}/posts";

        return await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url, cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool(ReadOnly = false, Destructive = false),
     Description("Create a new post for a user.")]
    public async Task<string> CreatePost(
        [Description("ID of the user authoring the post")] int userId,
        [Description("Post title")] string title,
        [Description("Post body text")] string body,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var payload = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { userId, title, body });
        var content = new StringContent(payload, System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

        var response = await httpClient.PostAsync($"{BaseUrl}/posts", content, cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

## How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

| Style | Class type | How services arrive |
|-------|-----------|-------------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static class (shown above) | Services in constructor params — resolved once per tool invocation |
| **Method parameter injection** | Static class | DI services as method params alongside tool params |

**Key rules:**
- `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<…>`, and **any DI-registered service** are **automatically injected** — they never appear in the tool's JSON schema that the LLM sees.
- Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the LLM.
- `builder.Services.AddHttpClient()` registers both `IHttpClientFactory` and `HttpClient` for injection. `ILogger<T>` is registered by default.
- `.WithToolsFromAssembly()` uses reflection to discover all `[McpServerToolType]` classes. For Native AOT, use `.WithTools<ApiTools>()` instead.

### Scaffold shortcut

If starting from scratch:

```bash
dotnet new install Microsoft.McpServer.ProjectTemplates
dotnet new mcpserver -n MyApiServer
```

Then replace the generated tool class with your `ApiTools` and update `Program.cs` to register `AddHttpClient()`.
